NAME
    fs - the Y1/OS file API for programs (#include "../lib_fs.c")

SYNOPSIS
    #include "../lib_fs.c"        (includes lib_abi.c)
    #include "y1lib.c"

DESCRIPTION
    C wrappers, one sys() call each, around the Y1/OS syscalls, so a
    command never sees a number or an address; y1cc drops the ones a
    command does not call. Every result is a full 16-bit word: a
    handle 1..4 or 0, 1/0 for done/cannot, a count, or a byte with
    65535 for none (the end of a file, Ctrl-D). There are four
    handles, each with its own 512-byte buffer.

    Reading
        fopen(path)             a read handle; 0 = not found, a
                                directory, 16M or more, none free
        fgetc(h)                the next byte, 65535 at the end
        fread(h, buf)           the next whole sector into buf (512),
                                the byte count, 0 at the end; mix with
                                fgetc only at sector boundaries
        freadn(h, buf, n)       up to n bytes into buf, never past the
                                end of the position's sector; the
                                count, 0 at the end; mixes with fgetc
                                (a buffer of your own: one syscall a
                                block, not one a byte)
        fseek(h, hi, lo)        a read handle to position hi:lo (24
                                bits), not past the end; 1 = done
        fclose(h)
    Writing (one file open for writing at a time)
        fcreate(path, load, exec)   a handle; a same-named file is
                                    replaced when it is closed; 0
                                    while another write is open, and
                                    so inside a > or a pipe (shell)
        fputc(h, c)  fwrite(h, buf, n)  fputs(h, s)
        fclose(h)               registers the file; 1 = ok
    Names
        fdelete(path)  fmkdir(path)  frmdir(path)  chdir(path)
        frename(path, newname)  a bare name, same directory
        getcwd(buf)             the current path (64 bytes); its length
    Entries
        opendir(path)           a directory handle ("" = current)
        readdir(h, ent)         1 with the next live 32-byte entry
        fresolve(path, ent)     1 found, the entry in ent (or ent 0)
        fentry(ent)             the entry the last call found
        ent_isfile ent_isdir ent_lba ent_load ent_exec (e)
        ent_len(e)              the low 16 bits; e[18] counts 64Ks
        ent_lenx(e)             bits 16-23 (e[18]): files are up to
                                16M - 1 (24-bit positions, 2026-09-25)
        ent_name(e, out)        the name, unpadded (out: 13 bytes)
    Console
        conin()                 DATA: the next byte of stdin, no echo
                                (a < file or a pipe, else the
                                console); 65535 at its end / Ctrl-D
        constat()               1 when a stdin byte is waiting
        keyin()                 a KEY: always the keyboard, no echo,
                                never redirected; 65535 at Ctrl-D
        stdio()                 bit 0 stdin, bit 1 stdout redirected
    Output is putchar/puts (y1cc --os: the CONOUT syscall, which the
    shell redirects); errors are eputs() from lib_err.c (the screen).
    Arguments
        argword(tail, out, max) the next word of a command tail into
                                out (max characters); returns the rest
    Programs (2026-09-25)
        osexit(status)          end the program now, from anywhere (its
                                own --stack too); the next program finds
                                status at STATUS ($0F0E: peekw)
        osexec(path, args)      end this program and run path with args
                                as its tail (same command: > and | stay);
                                returns 0 only when path cannot run

    Paths are absolute or relative to the current directory; names are
    1..12 characters, case-sensitive. What a program leaves open the
    shell closes when it returns (a pending write is registered).

EXAMPLES
    h = fopen(argstr());                       a one-file cat
    if (!h) { puts("not found"); return; }
    while ((c = fgetc(h)) != 65535) putchar(c);
    fclose(h);

NOTES
    New in Y1/OS 2026-09-23. It replaces the P8X BIOS file calls
    (FRESOLVE, FOPEN, FGETB, FWOPEN, FPUTB, FNEXT...) with their carry
    tests, the RDBUF page, and P8X os/commands/lib_dirent.c.

